<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Proconsul</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as proconsuls</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A proconsul was a Roman official who exercised the authority of a consul outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, usually as governor of a province. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, senatorial provinces were governed by proconsuls, nominally under the Senate's control. Imperial provinces, by contrast, were governed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legatus">legates</a> appointed by the emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/proconsul.jpg" alt="Proconsul" width="320" height="376" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergey Sosnovskiy · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Sulla_(Ny_Carlsberg_Glyptotek,_Copenhagen)_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> word prōconsul is a shortened form of prō consule, meaning one acting on behalf of the consul, and it appears on inscriptions from 135 BCE. The office was created to deal with a constitutional peculiarity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>: only a consul could command an army, but two consuls served at a time, each for one year, and could not normally serve two terms running. If a campaign was under way when a consul's term ended, his command could be prorogued, which kept continuity.</p><p>Ancient historians describe Quintus Publilius Philo as the first proconsul. His army was besieging Neapolis when his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">consulship</a> expired, and the people voted that he should conduct the campaign in place of a consul.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>A proconsul held full consular authority outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> noted that he lacked the right to take the auspices. Proconsulships were in practice often treated as extensions of a consul's term. Two proconsuls of the Republic had never been consul: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a>, made proconsul by vote of the Popular Assembly to lead the expedition against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthaginians</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and Lucius Manlius Acidinus.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> annexed territory, proconsuls and propraetors became its provincial governors, assigned the provinces by lot, the proconsuls those with the most troops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> made the governorships annual in 81 BCE and required a governor to leave the province within thirty days of his successor's arrival. In 67 BCE Pompey received a multiyear proconsulship, which had no precedent, to deal with piracy.</p><p>Augustus replaced the armies loyal to individual commanders with a standing imperial army. Proconsuls lost military authority but kept the prestige of the title. He kept the border provinces, where most legions stood, under his own control, and the governors of senatorial provinces were chosen by lot, with the result ratified by the Senate. A passage in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> notes that cases might be judged by a proconsul. An early fifth-century document, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/notitia-dignitatum">Notitia Dignitatum</a>, lists three: for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Although proconsul was an official title only in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the word has been applied to modern British, American and French officials with delegated authority. Writers such as Carnes Lord discuss figures like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonard-wood">Leonard Wood</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, Lucius D. Clay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and Paul Bremer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 2003 within the framework of proconsular authority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Proconsul” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Provinces were classed as either senatorial (governed by <b>proconsuls</b>, nominally under Senate control) or imperial (governed by legates appointed by and answerable to the emperor), while Egypt, strategically vital and critical to the grain supply, was placed under an equestrian governor, the Praefectus Aegypti, of proven personal loyalty.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
